// A fraction of 16 digits is not a fraction a digit-accumulating parser can
// round. `0.9999999999999999` has the numerator 9999999999999999, which is
// above 2^53, so a parser that reads the fraction into one Number and divides
// by 10^16 rounds the numerator to 1e16 and returns 1.0 (issue #772). The
// nearest double is the value the literal spells.
//
// The same fault reaches the index rule: a fractional List index is the
// catchable `IndexNotIntegerError` of spec/04-semantics.md, Index Get step 4b,
// so a consumer that rounds the literal to 1 prints `8` here instead of the
// kind. The fault is inside a `try` because the spec makes it catchable; an
// uncaught one halts (spec/04-semantics.md, Halting Faults).

print 0.9999999999999999;
print 0.9999999999999999 == 1;
try {
    print [7, 8][0.9999999999999999];
} catch (e) {
    print e.kind;
}

// The numerator 9007199254740993 is 2^53 + 1, which a parser that keeps the
// fraction in a Number cannot hold. This is the same defect without a run of
// nines, so the probe is not one value's shape.

print 0.9007199254740993;
print 0.9007199254740993 == 0.9007199254740992;

// STATUS: ok
// CHECK: 0.9999999999999999
// CHECK: false
// CHECK: IndexNotIntegerError
// CHECK: 0.9007199254740993
// CHECK: false
